    <h3>Cap Rate vs. Cash-on-Cash Return: What is the Difference?</h3>
    <p>Many novice investors confuse Cap Rate with Cash-on-Cash Return, but they answer two completely different financial questions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cap Rate evaluates the asset:</strong> It measures the unleveraged annual return of the real estate as if you bought it with 100% cash. It allows you to compare a property in Texas directly against one in Ohio without the skew of different interest rates or loan terms.</li>
      <li><strong>Cash-on-Cash Return evaluates your financial leverage:</strong> It measures the actual cash return you receive on the actual dollars wired out of your bank account (down payment plus closing costs). When interest rates are lower than your cap rate, leverage magnifies your return.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Planning for Capital Expenditures (CapEx)</h3>
    <p>Routine maintenance (fixing a running toilet or repairing a disposal) is distinct from Capital Expenditures (CapEx). CapEx represents long-term replacement reserves for major structural components. Failing to set aside $150 to $250 per unit each month for CapEx is the most common reason small rental businesses fail when an HVAC unit ($6,000&ndash;$9,000) or shingle roof ($8,000&ndash;$14,000) requires emergency replacement.</p>
